>

  <article class="section-container py-12 max-w-[760px]">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-warm-900 mb-6">{title}</h1>
    <p class="text-xs text-zinc-500 mb-6">Reading time: 7 minutes</p>

    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">It's one of the most confusing facts in tea: green tea and white tea both come from the <em>Camellia sinensis</em> plant, and neither one is oxidized. Yet a good Longjing and a good Silver Needle could not taste more different. The difference is entirely in what happens after the leaves are picked — and it tells you which one you want.</p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">What happens in the first two hours</h2>
    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The moment a leaf is picked, enzymes inside it begin oxidizing — the same process that browns a cut apple. Everything that follows is a race to stop or manage that oxidation.</p>
    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-6"><strong class="text-warm-900">Green tea</strong> is heat-treated within hours of picking. Chinese greens are pan-fired in wok-like drums at high heat, which kills the enzymes and locks in the leaf's green color and vegetal freshness. That heat is why Chinese green teas taste toasty and nutty — Longjing's signature chestnut note is literally cooked into the leaf.</p>
    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-6"><strong class="text-warm-900">White tea</strong> never gets heat near it. It's simply withered — spread out and left to slowly lose moisture — and then gently dried. No rolling, no firing, no oxidation control. The leaf keeps more of what it is: delicate, naturally sweet, almost fragile. That's why white tea is often called the least processed tea in the world.</p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">What it means in the cup</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-8">
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-2">Green tea</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-muted list-disc pl-5 leading-relaxed">
          <li>Toasty, nutty, sometimes grassy or chestnut</li>
          <li>Medium caffeine (25-35mg per cup)</li>
          <li>Needs careful water temperature — boiling water burns it</li>
          <li>Best within 6-12 months of harvest</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-2">White tea</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-muted list-disc pl-5 leading-relaxed">
          <li>Sweet, melon, hay, almost floral — zero bitterness</li>
          <li>Lowest caffeine of any tea (15-25mg)</li>
          <li>Nearly impossible to over-brew</li>
          <li>Aged whites are a category of their own — they evolve for decades</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which should you start with?</h2>
    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">If you already drink coffee or black tea, start with <strong class="text-warm-900">green tea</strong> — try <a href="/teas/longjing/" class="text-accent underline">Longjing</a> for the classic toasty-chestnut profile, or <a href="/teas/anji-bai-cha/" class="text-accent underline">Anji Bai Cha</a> for a green that's almost unnaturally smooth.</p>
    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">If you're new to tea entirely, sensitive to caffeine, or think you don't like tea, start with <strong class="text-warm-900">white</strong> — <a href="/teas/silver-needle/" class="text-accent underline">Silver Needle</a> is the purest expression of the leaf there is, and it's the most forgiving tea to brew on the planet.</p>
    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">And if you can't choose? You don't have to. A morning Longjing and an afternoon Silver Needle is a completely reasonable daily routine — they're different moods of the same plant.</p>

    <div class="card p-6 bg-leaf-50 border-leaf-200 mb-8">
      <p class="text-sm text-accent leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-leaf-900">One naming trap:</strong> Anji Bai Cha is called "white tea" (白茶) but is a green tea — the name refers to its pale albino leaves, not the processing. A genuine white tea like Silver Needle is never pan-fired.</p>
    </div>
